{
  "slug": "max-area-of-island",
  "title": "Max Area of Island",
  "topic": "graphs",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "graph traversal (iterative flood fill)",
  "statement": "You are given a rectangular matrix in which every cell is `0` (water) or `1` (land). With m rows and n columns, two land cells belong to the same island when they can be linked through a chain of land cells that touch horizontally or vertically (diagonal contact does not count). The area of an island is the number of cells it contains. Return the area of the largest island, or 0 when the matrix holds no land at all.\n\nFor the grid `[[1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 0], [0, 0, 1]]` the land cells `(0, 0)`, `(1, 0)` and `(1, 1)` touch edge-to-edge and form one island of three cells, while `(2, 2)` is a lone island of one, so the answer is 3.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= grid.length <= 50`, `1 <= grid[i].length <= 50` and `0 <= grid[i][j] <= 1`. Every row has the same length.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "grid = [[0]]",
      "output": "0",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "grid = [[1]]",
      "output": "1",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(grid: number[][])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "The largest island has to be measured, so you need a way to visit every cell of one island without leaking into another.",
    "A flood fill from a single land cell measures that island in time proportional to its area; the only danger is measuring the same island again and again.",
    "Scan the matrix once and start a traversal only from a land cell you have not marked as visited yet; then every cell participates in at most one traversal."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force never marks an island as finished. It gives every land cell a unique label, then repeatedly sweeps the whole matrix pulling the smallest label of any land neighbour into the current cell, restarting the sweep whenever some label changed. Each full sweep is O(m*n) and the labels keep moving for up to O(m+n) sweeps, so the brute costs O(m*n*(m+n)) time and O(m*n) space. The optimal visits each island exactly once: it scans the cells in order, and whenever it meets a land cell that has not been marked yet it runs an iterative stack-based flood fill from it, marking every reachable land cell as visited while counting them, then keeps the largest count seen.",
    "why_optimal": "The outer scan looks at each cell once and the flood fill claims each reachable land cell once, so the whole function is O(m*n) time, which is the floor because the entire matrix has to be read at least once, with O(m*n) space for the visited set. The brute reselects the same cells across many full-matrix sweeps, which is why the same work is repeated a factor of O(m+n) times. Marking a cell when it is pushed rather than when it is popped is what keeps the total work linear.",
    "pitfalls": "Marking a neighbour only when it is popped instead of when it is pushed lets the same cell enter the stack many times and quadratically inflates the count on a solid block of land. Using recursion for the flood fill hits the default recursion limit on a 50x50 block. Reading `grid[r + 1][c]` for a vertical neighbour without checking that row's length raises an index error whenever the row is shorter. A matrix with no land at all must return 0 and a single land cell must return 1; treating diagonal neighbours as connected is also wrong."
  },
